Likutay Halachos — Kesuvos §2

:root {

--ink: #1a1208;

--parchment: #f5edd6;

--gold: #b8860b;

--deep-gold: #8b6914;

--crimson: #8b1a1a;

--deep-green: #1a4a1a;

--blue-note: #1a3a6b;

--border: #c8a84b;

* { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

body {

background: #2a1f0a;

background-image:

radial-gradient(ellipse at 20% 20%, rgba(184,134,11,0.15) 0%, transparent 60%),

radial-gradient(ellipse at 80% 80%, rgba(139,26,26,0.1) 0%, transparent 60%);

min-height: 100vh;

padding: 2rem 1rem;

font-family: 'Palatino Linotype', Palatino, 'Book Antiqua', Georgia, serif;

color: var(--ink);

.page-container { max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto; }

.lang-toggle {

display: flex; gap: 0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;

border: 2px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 4px;

overflow: hidden; width: fit-content;

.lang-toggle button {

padding: 0.5rem 1.4rem; background: transparent; border: none;

cursor: pointer; font-family: inherit; font-size: 0.9rem;

color: #c8a84b; transition: all 0.2s; letter-spacing: 0.04em;

.lang-toggle button.active { background: var(--gold); color: #1a1208; font-weight: bold; }

.lang-toggle button:hover:not(.active) { background: rgba(184,134,11,0.2); }

.document {

background: var(--parchment);

border: 3px solid var(--border); border-radius: 2px;

box-shadow: 0 8px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.6), inset 0 0 60px rgba(184,134,11,0.05);

padding: 2.5rem 3rem; position: relative;

.document::before {

content: ''; position: absolute;

top: 8px; left: 8px; right: 8px; bottom: 8px;

border: 1px solid rgba(184,134,11,0.3); pointer-events: none;

.book-title { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.85rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--deep-gold); font-style: italic; }

.section-title { text-align: center; font-size: 1.6rem; color: var(--crimson); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; letter-spacing: 0.03em; }

.halachic-ruling {

background: rgba(184,134,11,0.1);

border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-right: 4px solid var(--gold);

padding: 0.8rem 1.2rem; margin: 1.2rem 0;

font-style: italic; font-size: 1.02rem; color: #2a1a0a;

text-align: center; border-radius: 2px;

p { line-height: 1.85; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.0rem; text-align: justify; hyphens: auto; }

.trans { font-style: italic; color: #2a1a00; }

.xref { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--blue-note); font-style: italic; }

.note { font-size: 0.82rem; color: #555; font-style: italic; }

.source-attr { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--deep-green); font-style: italic; text-align: center; }

b { color: var(--crimson); }

em { color: #2a3a6b; }

.divider { border: none; border-top: 1px dashed var(--border); margin: 1.5rem 0; }

.xref-footer {

margin-top: 1.5rem;

background: rgba(184,134,11,0.07);

border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 2px;

padding: 0.8rem 1.2rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #3a2a0a;

font-style: italic;

.summary-box {

margin-top: 2.5rem;

background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a2a1a 0%, #0a1a2a 100%);

border: 2px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 4px;

padding: 1.5rem 2rem; color: #e8dfc0;

.summary-box h3 { color: var(--gold); font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(184,134,11,0.4); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }

.summary-box p { color: #d8cfb0; font-size: 0.95rem; text-align: left; }

.hebrew-block { display: none; }

.english-block { display: block; }

body.show-hebrew .hebrew-block { display: block; }

body.show-hebrew .english-block { display: none; }

body.show-both .hebrew-block { display: block; }

body.show-both .english-block { display: block; }

@media (max-width: 600px) {

.document { padding: 1.5rem 1.2rem; }

.section-title { font-size: 1.3rem; }

English

Likutay Halachos — Even Hu'ezer

Kesuvos [Laws of the Marriage Contract] — §2

The matter of the kesuvah — based on what Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, wrote in the teaching "Kartalisah" in Likutay Moharan Part One (§18). See the entire teaching there.

For everything has a tachlis (purpose, ultimate completion), and the tachlis is bound to the machshavah (the original thought) and is closer to the machshavah than the intermediate action from which the tachlis comes — in the dimension of sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah (the end of the deed was in the beginning of the thought) (from the Lecha Dodi liturgy). And the principal tachlis of all Creation is the delight of the World to Come. This tachlis can only be attained — tzaddikim alone attain this tachlis — each one according to his root within the soul of the tzaddik, from which he receives this tachlis in proportion to his ability to transform anger into compassion, and so forth.

And the general principle is: through the tzaddikim receiving the dimension of Malchus (Kingship) and leadership, the tzaddikim thereby know the tachlis. For through their grasp of the dimension of Malchus, they thereby hold fast to the wholeness of the letters within Malchus, and through holding fast to the complete letters, they thereby hold fast to the tachlis — which is the completeness of the letters, which is the dimension of Yud, the dimension of the World to Come, and so forth. See there carefully.

And this is the dimension of "B'chol derachecha da'eihu" — in all your ways, know Him (Mishlei 3:6) — through which a person can attain the tachlis in everything. For every thing was created through letters, and so forth. And the tachlis is called tekheles (the blue-violet color of the tzitzis thread), for tekheles is a blend of black and white — like writing, which is black upon white; certainly the underside of the letter, and so forth — for so it is that the tachlis, which is the end of the deed (sof ha'ma'aseh), is in the beginning of the thought (machshavah techilah) and is close to the thought, as above. See all of this there very carefully, for these are exceedingly deep matters.
